Apple Core is a hard-fork of mattt/iMCP (https://github.com/mattt/iMCP),
licensed MIT, Copyright 2025 Mattt (https://mat.tt). iMCP's original
source files are preserved with their MIT copyright headers intact under
the one-way MIT-into-GPL compatibility described in
docs/planning/BUILD_PLAN.md §4. The full text of iMCP's license is kept
at THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/iMCP.LICENSE.

The **signed-binary distribution channel** (the prebuilt DMG Oliver sells
via Gumroad) is offered under `EULA.md`, not the GPL. It contains no
GPL-licensed third-party code, so its conveyance creates no GPL offer.
The published source — the thing a GPL fork rebuilds from — remains
GPL-3.0-or-later; the GPL at `THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/iMCP.LICENSE` is not
the license on a bought binary. `apple-mail-mcp`'s GPL code is therefore
clean-room reference only: new code must be written from the documented
behavior (see `docs/planning/BUILD_PLAN.md` §4.4 and
`docs/licensing.md`).

Serving infrastructure (HTTP/SSE server, OAuth support, Cloudflare
tunnel management, LaunchAgent daemon registration) is ported from
Bridgeport (https://github.com/oliverames/bridgeport), a separate
personal project also authored by Oliver Ames. No third-party attribution
applies to this code.

The Messages recipient-normalization behavior was designed from patterns
documented by Dhravya Shah's apple-mcp project
(https://github.com/supermemoryai/apple-mcp), licensed MIT, Copyright
2025 Dhravya Shah. Its license is preserved at
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/apple-mcp.LICENSE.

The Messages send-via-Messages.app behavior was independently implemented
after studying Carter Lasalle's mac_messages_mcp project
(https://github.com/carterlasalle/mac_messages_mcp), licensed MIT,
Copyright 2023 Carter Lasalle. Its license is preserved at
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/mac_messages_mcp.LICENSE.

The Notes surface is designed against Rob Sweet's apple-notes-mcp project
(https://github.com/sweetrb/apple-notes-mcp), licensed MIT, Copyright
2025 Rob Sweet. Its tool surface is the reference Apple Core's Notes
tools are derived from, including which tools exist and what each one
returns, and the NoteStore.sqlite reads in Shared/NotesDatabaseReader.swift
follow its documented approach — in particular the protobuf field path
used to recover checklist done-state, which is Apple's own undocumented
format as charted by that project. Its license is preserved at
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/apple-notes-mcp.LICENSE.

Additional patterns and design research are drawn from the Apple-related
MCP server synthesis documented in docs/planning/DONORS.md and
docs/planning/SYNTHESIS.md. When another surface implementation actually
lifts code or a substantially-derived design from one of those projects,
this file and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/ must be updated to include:

  - The donor project's name, URL, and license
  - A copy of its license text in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/<project>.LICENSE
  - A one-line note here naming which surface/pattern was lifted

See docs/planning/BUILD_PLAN.md §4.2 for the full attribution discipline
(per-file SPDX headers, commit-log conventions) to follow when that work
happens.
